WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.621 --> 00:00:04.296
♪ In the arms of the angels ♪

00:00:04.296 --> 00:00:06.430
- We're all familiar with
the saddest ad of all time.

00:00:06.430 --> 00:00:07.810
But did you know that it helped raise

00:00:07.810 --> 00:00:10.097
over $30 million for the ASPCA?

00:00:10.941 --> 00:00:13.441
(light music)

00:00:15.763 --> 00:00:16.658
Hey, Nahre.

00:00:16.658 --> 00:00:17.921
- Hey, LA, what's up?

00:00:17.921 --> 00:00:19.430
(laughter)

00:00:19.430 --> 00:00:22.010
- Okay, so, I was watching the

00:00:22.010 --> 00:00:26.370
sad animal commercial
with Sarah McLachlan.

00:00:26.370 --> 00:00:28.653
In the arms of an angel.

00:00:30.038 --> 00:00:31.640
And it's the saddest thing ever.

00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:34.890
How did they make that commercial so sad?

00:00:34.890 --> 00:00:36.180
How did they get that effect?

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:40.200
I know it's complicated, but
could you explain the basics

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:43.420
of what makes a song sound sad to us?

00:00:43.420 --> 00:00:45.590
- You know, I think
that's just a topic where

00:00:45.590 --> 00:00:49.280
songwriters are really so
far ahead of scientists

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:54.280
that I feel hesitant to
dispense any suggestions.

00:00:54.310 --> 00:00:55.520
- So there are many techniques that

00:00:55.520 --> 00:00:58.010
songwriters use to make a song sound sad.

00:00:58.010 --> 00:00:59.530
Although with all of these techniques,

00:00:59.530 --> 00:01:01.430
there are plenty of exceptions.

00:01:01.430 --> 00:01:04.480
Stereotypically speaking,
people associate major chords

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:07.780
with happiness and minor
chords with sadness.

00:01:07.780 --> 00:01:10.090
But oftentimes this isn't the case.

00:01:10.090 --> 00:01:12.560
For example, "Angel" by Sarah McLachlan

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:15.268
is in the key of D flat major.

00:01:15.268 --> 00:01:20.268
♪ In the arms of the angel ♪

00:01:20.698 --> 00:01:23.865
♪ Fly away from here. ♪

00:01:25.990 --> 00:01:27.620
- On the other hand, there plenty of

00:01:27.620 --> 00:01:30.100
party anthems written in the minor key,

00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:32.466
and they definitely don't sound sad.

00:01:32.466 --> 00:01:33.960
♪ Every day I'm shuffling ♪

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:38.000
♪ Party rockers in the house tonight ♪

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:42.700
♪ We just wanna see ya shake that ♪

00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:44.530
- But still here are some
ingredients that can help

00:01:44.530 --> 00:01:48.120
you write a sad song, including
the use of minor chords.

00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:49.810
Western music is generally based on

00:01:49.810 --> 00:01:52.010
the relationship between
the following chords.

00:01:52.010 --> 00:01:53.543
Here it is in C major.

00:01:54.437 --> 00:01:56.937
(plays piano)

00:01:59.090 --> 00:02:00.962
Now in C minor.

00:02:00.962 --> 00:02:03.462
(plays piano)

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:09.090
Let's take the C minor chord
in its most basic form.

00:02:09.090 --> 00:02:12.283
It's comprised of three
notes, C, E flat, and G.

00:02:14.180 --> 00:02:16.410
If I play a note that it
is not part of this chord,

00:02:16.410 --> 00:02:18.053
it's called a non chord tone.

00:02:18.980 --> 00:02:22.821
For example, F is not a
part of these three notes.

00:02:22.821 --> 00:02:24.710
(plays piano)

00:02:24.710 --> 00:02:27.210
Now if I play this F and
I bring it back to one of

00:02:27.210 --> 00:02:30.422
the chord tones, in this case, E flat.

00:02:30.422 --> 00:02:32.700
(plays piano)

00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:34.703
This movement is called an appoggiatura.

00:02:35.686 --> 00:02:37.080
(plays piano)

00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:38.880
Applied to the previous chord progression,

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:40.343
they sound like this.

00:02:40.343 --> 00:02:42.843
(plays piano)

00:02:47.313 --> 00:02:51.430
Let's now add some more notes
to create descending lines.

00:02:51.430 --> 00:02:53.250
This type of movement downwards tends

00:02:53.250 --> 00:02:55.452
to create a sense of longing.

00:02:55.452 --> 00:02:57.952
(plays piano)

00:03:03.340 --> 00:03:05.110
Repeated notes and figures can also

00:03:05.110 --> 00:03:07.210
create a more somber effect.

00:03:07.210 --> 00:03:09.796
For example, let's compare this figure.

00:03:09.796 --> 00:03:12.296
(plays piano)

00:03:14.810 --> 00:03:15.643
To this one.

00:03:16.477 --> 00:03:18.977
(plays piano)

00:03:23.930 --> 00:03:26.410
Now, of course a lot of the
emotional effects that come from

00:03:26.410 --> 00:03:29.625
these notes depend on the
way that you play them.

00:03:29.625 --> 00:03:32.125
(plays piano)

00:03:45.186 --> 00:03:46.610
- One of the main ingredients for

00:03:46.610 --> 00:03:49.370
a sad song would be a slow tempo.

00:03:49.370 --> 00:03:52.190
- One thing people think
about has to do with

00:03:52.190 --> 00:03:57.020
how we move and speak when we're sad.

00:03:57.020 --> 00:04:00.960
And this idea that music kind of borrows

00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:04.330
or mimics those characteristics.

00:04:04.330 --> 00:04:09.030
So if you think about what
it would sound like if I was

00:04:09.030 --> 00:04:12.940
talking to you and I was really sad.

00:04:12.940 --> 00:04:13.773
(laughter)

00:04:13.773 --> 00:04:15.056
Right?

00:04:15.056 --> 00:04:17.210
I might slow down, my
voice might get lower.

00:04:17.210 --> 00:04:20.460
And so all these kinds of things
that you might do musically

00:04:20.460 --> 00:04:22.870
when you're trying to express sadness.

00:04:22.870 --> 00:04:24.990
- If you were to speed up the song "Angel"

00:04:24.990 --> 00:04:28.617
by Sarah McLachlan, it
wouldn't sound very sad at all.

00:04:29.496 --> 00:04:33.366
♪ In the arms of the
angel fly away from here ♪

00:04:33.366 --> 00:04:35.369
♪ From this dark cold hotel room ♪

00:04:35.369 --> 00:04:37.922
♪ And the endlessness that you feel ♪

00:04:37.922 --> 00:04:40.400
♪ From this dark cold hotel room ♪

00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:42.320
- So, "Angel" has a slow
tempo, and there are

00:04:42.320 --> 00:04:44.370
some repeated notes and chord movements

00:04:44.370 --> 00:04:46.770
that create some tension,
but overall I couldn't

00:04:46.770 --> 00:04:50.130
find that many sad song
techniques used in the song.

00:04:50.130 --> 00:04:51.810
So I wonder, is the commercial sad

00:04:51.810 --> 00:04:54.020
because of the song itself, or because of

00:04:54.020 --> 00:04:55.930
the visuals that go along with it?

00:04:55.930 --> 00:04:59.430
- There are lots of
studies in music perception

00:04:59.430 --> 00:05:01.077
that ask exactly that kind of question.

00:05:01.077 --> 00:05:03.460
And the way they'll ask
it is they'll play people

00:05:03.460 --> 00:05:06.210
just the song and then
they'll show other people

00:05:06.210 --> 00:05:09.030
just the imagery, just the sad dogs.

00:05:09.030 --> 00:05:12.130
And they'll show other
people the whole commercial

00:05:12.130 --> 00:05:15.100
and get people to rate how sad it seems.

00:05:15.100 --> 00:05:19.840
And usually the type of
thing that research finds

00:05:19.840 --> 00:05:22.760
is that those visual images are really

00:05:22.760 --> 00:05:26.070
seeping into the sounds themselves.

00:05:26.070 --> 00:05:28.520
You feel like you're
responding to the music,

00:05:28.520 --> 00:05:32.720
but it's really quite
impactfully shaped by the imagery

00:05:32.720 --> 00:05:34.430
you're seeing while you're listening.

00:05:34.430 --> 00:05:36.850
And not only for that
hearing, but much later.

00:05:36.850 --> 00:05:39.410
So you could hear that song, you know,

00:05:39.410 --> 00:05:42.860
years later when you've
forgotten the sad dogs,

00:05:42.860 --> 00:05:45.860
like, you don't remember overtly the dogs.

00:05:45.860 --> 00:05:48.940
However, you know, they've
left their mark, in the sense

00:05:48.940 --> 00:05:52.896
that the song continues to
sound really devastating to you.

00:05:52.896 --> 00:05:54.720
- Is there something
that happens to the brain

00:05:54.720 --> 00:05:57.660
when you're processing
these types of emotions,

00:05:57.660 --> 00:05:59.650
when you're listening to something sad?

00:05:59.650 --> 00:06:02.280
- [Jonna] There's something
called mirror neurons

00:06:02.280 --> 00:06:05.050
or the mirror system in the brain.

00:06:05.050 --> 00:06:07.640
So these are basically neurons
in the brain that are active

00:06:07.640 --> 00:06:10.910
both when are observing
another person carrying out

00:06:10.910 --> 00:06:14.060
an action, such as grasping
an apple, for example,

00:06:14.060 --> 00:06:16.680
and when ourselves are grasping an apple.

00:06:16.680 --> 00:06:18.880
So basically these are
neurons that help us

00:06:18.880 --> 00:06:22.380
understand the actions
of others by actually

00:06:22.380 --> 00:06:25.850
stimulating them internally,
so as if we ourselves

00:06:25.850 --> 00:06:28.800
were carrying out that same action.

00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:30.270
This is also related to

00:06:30.270 --> 00:06:33.940
a phenomenon called emotional contagion.

00:06:33.940 --> 00:06:37.740
For example, if we are speaking
with someone who is sad,

00:06:37.740 --> 00:06:41.180
we start to mimic or imitate,

00:06:41.180 --> 00:06:45.280
mirror their sad facial
expressions and their posture,

00:06:45.280 --> 00:06:47.920
and we might ourselves kind of

00:06:47.920 --> 00:06:50.390
start feeling sad as a result.

00:06:50.390 --> 00:06:53.040
- And can this happen when
you're listening to music?

00:06:53.040 --> 00:06:55.990
Can you mirror what's happening
in the music, and, say that

00:06:55.990 --> 00:06:59.860
I'm happy today and I listened
to something that's sad.

00:06:59.860 --> 00:07:01.620
Can I all of a sudden turn sad

00:07:01.620 --> 00:07:04.230
because I'm mirroring these emotions?

00:07:04.230 --> 00:07:06.740
- We don't really have any
solid evidence on it yet,

00:07:06.740 --> 00:07:10.025
but there are quite a
lot of theoretical papers

00:07:10.025 --> 00:07:13.050
proposing that this is probably
what might be happening

00:07:13.050 --> 00:07:15.530
while are listening to sad
music as well, that our brain

00:07:15.530 --> 00:07:17.890
is engaged in this sort of mirroring.

00:07:17.890 --> 00:07:20.220
- So, we thought it could
be fun if we tried to make

00:07:20.220 --> 00:07:22.480
the music psychologist
sad by putting all of

00:07:22.480 --> 00:07:25.550
our sad ingredients
into one original song.

00:07:25.550 --> 00:07:27.300
And of course, we couldn't forget

00:07:27.300 --> 00:07:30.640
the saddest ingredient
of all: homeless animals.

00:07:32.023 --> 00:07:34.356
(sad music)

00:07:41.310 --> 00:07:44.520
- First of all there's this
sort of lamenting pattern,

00:07:44.520 --> 00:07:48.483
this very old kind of pattern
that's like a sigh, right?

00:07:48.483 --> 00:07:52.200
And there's also this kind of
stasis, right, in that excerpt

00:07:52.200 --> 00:07:54.940
where it feels like you're kind of stuck

00:07:54.940 --> 00:07:59.940
and that's a very kind
of common experience

00:08:00.140 --> 00:08:01.760
during sadness or depression, right?

00:08:01.760 --> 00:08:04.440
That you can't kind of move beyond it.

00:08:04.440 --> 00:08:08.427
I guess that's probably, you
know, not as helpful to you,

00:08:08.427 --> 00:08:12.010
but it's very helpful to me,
to like listen to, you know,

00:08:12.010 --> 00:08:14.664
what people do when they're
writing sad music, sure.

00:08:14.664 --> 00:08:18.830
♪ You're in the arms of the angels ♪

00:08:18.830 --> 00:08:20.840
- Hi, I'm Nahre Sol.

00:08:20.840 --> 00:08:23.746
Please say you'll
subscribe to Sound Field.

00:08:23.746 --> 00:08:25.060
We need your help.

00:08:25.060 --> 00:08:27.160
Right now, comment and
tell us what you think

00:08:27.160 --> 00:08:29.390
the saddest song in the world is,

00:08:29.390 --> 00:08:31.910
and let us know what you think of ours.

00:08:31.910 --> 00:08:34.823
And remember, adopt, don't shop.

